# Whole V3 plus single-owner publication: focused frontier admission — 22 October 2026

**Decision: do not admit this combined V3 variant as the next product wave.** The predeclared rule required a credible, block-supported improvement in **both** dense complete operations, with no credible regression in either guard. The one frozen eight-block comparison shows ordinary overlap in both dense operations: each candidate is faster in four corresponding blocks and slower in four. Neither dense operation establishes a repeatable gain. Preserve the correct whole variant at experiment commit `8f62b707f69896848154cfe4463c4f69a557b37e`; this focused result does not authorize the full main/published acceptance sequence or a selective rerun.

The comparison used the frozen single-owner reference archive SHA-256 `06fe22b7f05dceb27f59b2e82d33fab541c8cee04c37b1a1ccdf078f6ecdc9be` and combined archive SHA-256 `383fcbdd1cc12e59a4e563e55527aaa1f3f31e442b8ebfaaf26802063b4990bd`. The [predeclared protocol](v3-combination-protocol.json) specified exactly four unchanged complete callbacks, eight blocks, single-call sampling, and fixed-20 warmup (21 untimed calls per block in the harness). The [freeze](v3-combined/freeze.json) ties the clean experiment commit and built diff to the package, named Wasm, and harness inputs. The comparison [manifest](v3-combined/frontier-comparison/manifest.json) reports four matched rows, the declared archives and harness digest `c9f312d3b57cde2ed49b526955cf9f7eb6646e4f757dd5281a2d49005b4c0169`. Its baseline checks and timing modes have no reported mismatch. One comparison command exited successfully; no timing run was repeated for this review.

Pooled raw single-call percentiles below are absolute milliseconds, computed by nearest rank. `n` is the retained sample count for each arm; the [absolute export](v3-combined/frontier-absolute-results.json) preserves every block median and calibration diagnostic, while [baseline](v3-combined/frontier-comparison/baseline.json) and [candidate](v3-combined/frontier-comparison/candidate.json) preserve all raw samples and tails.

| Complete callback | Reference n; p50 / p90 / p95 ms | V3 n; p50 / p90 / p95 ms | Block support |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Rewrite 512 labels in one group | 184; 8.325208 / 8.803042 / 8.944167 | 200; 8.337834 / 8.868584 / 9.068291 | V3 faster 4/8, slower 4/8 |
| Publish 1024 retained Text instances | 128; 17.213917 / 17.940000 / 18.062334 | 128; 17.176875 / 17.954166 / 18.107958 | V3 faster 4/8, slower 4/8 |
| First same-length scene publication among 1000 labels | 272; 1.675208 / 1.846500 / 1.895375 | 288; 1.697625 / 2.118250 / 4.633417 | One elevated V3 block; other seven overlap |
| First set in a one-label scene | 584; 0.120833 / 0.153709 / 0.165208 | 600; 0.126541 / 0.160625 / 0.183666 | Noisy and clock affected in both arms |

The dense 512 pooled p50 is 0.012626 ms higher with V3; the dense 1024 pooled p50 is 0.037042 ms lower. The comparison's block-median confidence intervals also straddle zero (dense 512: −0.8% to +2.2%; dense 1024: −1.1% to +1.2%). These observations do not satisfy a credible gain in both dense callbacks. The harness labels all four rows “neutral” under its separate 5% classification floor; that label is **not** the admission rule. There is no 5% gain requirement in this decision.

The sparse publication guard has one V3 block median of 2.911834 ms, against its reference block median of 1.653958 ms; the other seven V3 block medians range from 1.637917 to 1.691834 ms. Its V3 maximum raw sample is 18.835542 ms. The elevated tail remains in the record, but one block and the tail alone do not establish a credible guard regression or a cause. The one-label setter's V3 p50 is 0.005708 ms higher; both arms were flagged noisy and clock affected. The harness’s software no-op calibration estimates, displayed in GHz, ranged from 3.86–3.95 for the reference and 3.92–3.95 for V3; these are not measured hardware frequencies. Its comparison-resolution estimates were 2.0% and 1.7%, respectively. The setter's within-process spread was 17.0% and 16.6%, respectively. No samples or blocks were excluded by this review; the export reports no baseline check failures or timing-mode mismatches.

The source review found no actionable correctness defect, and the reported native/package correctness and named-Wasm gates passed before timing. Those gates establish a correct, authenticated experiment, not a speedup. The [source-cost correction](next-wave-selection-cost-correction.md) matters here: the existing runtime already makes one ordered rope traversal. V3 trades owner-range lookup and dispatch for an admission lookup/check, leaving both variants at O(N log P + G) owner/source-row work before shared rope, binding and Codec costs. The timed Wasm callback has no admission or row-count counters, so the measured overlap cannot be attributed to one internal step.

This is a focused choice against the frozen single-owner reference, not the seven-family comparison against current main and published 0.1.0. Candidate-specific API/ABI, owned-memory, browser, hero, and final size acceptance remain unverified for product admission. Keep the product freeze and draft stack held; preserve the experiment as an inconclusive correct whole variant under its ref. Stop this V3 selection path here under the declared protocol.
